Falsifies `paging::domain::the_enumeration_is_injective`.

The defect: the page index is truncated to 32 bits. A page index that fits in a `u32` is an
assumption worth about 16 TiB of grant and is the kind of thing that arrives from a neighbouring
interface rather than from bad arithmetic: descriptor counts, queue depths and DMA transfer
lengths are 16- or 32-bit fields in every device format in this tree. Under it, index `i` and
index `i + 2^32` enumerate the same IOVA.

Expected to fail: `assert_ne!(grant_page(r, i), grant_page(r, j), "two distinct indices
enumerate the same page")`, for `i = 0` and `j = 2^32` on any grant of at least 2^44 bytes,
which the harness reaches because its only assumptions are `i < pages`, `j < pages` and `i !=
j`, and `grant_pages` accepts a size that large.

Injectivity is the property with the least obvious consequence of the four, and it is here
because `Mapper::map` refuses a second write to a live leaf: without it a perfectly legal grant
fails its own build with `AlreadyMapped`, which reads as a caller error and is not one.

